Title: Turn off /SAFESEH for 32-bit Windows, which ICU's data object cannot satisfy
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

  lld-link : error : /safeseh:
  ../obj/global_intermediate/icudt78l_dat.obj is not compatible with SEH
  [.../gen-regexp-special-case.vcxproj]

x86 links default to /SAFESEH:YES, and that requires the linker to account for
every object in the image: each one must either register its exception
handlers or carry the @feat.00 symbol whose low bit says it has none. cl and ml
emit that symbol as a matter of course. genccode does not -- it writes the ICU
data object through its own COFF writer rather than through a compiler or an
assembler, so there is nothing in it for /SAFESEH to go on and the link is
refused.

Set on the ia32 condition in common.gypi rather than on the targets that link
the data, because by the time it bites that is most of them: node.exe, and
every build-time generator that pulls ICU in. gen-regexp-special-case is
merely the first to reach the link.

x86 only, and not because the other architectures are exempt by choice:
/SAFESEH does not exist on x64 or arm64 at all. Exception data lives in the PE
image there rather than in a table the linker has to vouch for, so there is
nothing to turn off.

This does give up a mitigation. The alternative is teaching genccode to emit
@feat.00, which is a small change in pkg_genc.cpp's object writer and would
keep /SAFESEH on for everything. It is the better fix if this build ever wants
the mitigation back; it is not the smaller one, and it edits a vendored
dependency.

diff --git a/common.gypi b/common.gypi
--- a/common.gypi
+++ b/common.gypi
@@ -407,6 +407,30 @@
         'conditions': [
           ['target_arch=="ia32"', {
             'TargetMachine' : 1,              # /MACHINE:X86
+            # /SAFESEH:NO. ICU's data object is the reason:
+            #
+            #   lld-link : error : /safeseh:
+            #   ../obj/global_intermediate/icudt78l_dat.obj
+            #   is not compatible with SEH
+            #
+            # genccode writes that object with its own COFF writer rather than
+            # through an assembler or a compiler, and so never emits the @feat.00
+            # symbol whose low bit declares a module free of SEH handlers. cl and
+            # ml both emit it; anything that does not is rejected outright,
+            # because /SAFESEH has to account for every object in the image.
+            #
+            # Set here rather than on the targets that link the data, because by
+            # the time it fails that is most of them: node.exe, and every
+            # build-time generator that pulls ICU in, starting with
+            # gen-regexp-special-case.
+            #
+            # x86 only. /SAFESEH does not exist on x64 or arm64, where exception
+            # data lives in the PE itself rather than in a table the linker has
+            # to vouch for.
+            #
+            # The alternative is teaching genccode to emit @feat.00, which keeps
+            # the mitigation on. Worth doing if this build ever wants it back.
+            'ImageHasSafeExceptionHandlers': 'false',
           }],
           ['target_arch=="x64"', {
             'TargetMachine' : 17,             # /MACHINE:X64
